content:
{
  "approved_angle": {
    "title": "I want to know what made a couple book, as well as what they loved afterward",
    "core_perspective": "I want to understand what a couple was weighing when they chose your business. Their review can tell us what they loved about the experience. Asking about the booking decision can give us different information to consider when we write your ads."
  },
  "sharpened_point": null,
  "strategic_purpose": {
    "pillar": "ATTRACT",
    "content_type": "founder_forward",
    "why_now": "Build founder authority within Watch Us Build an Ad Strategy by showing a specific question Whitney brings to advertising work. Give owners with strong reviews another useful perspective on what couples can tell them."
  },
  "audience": {
    "who": "Wedding business owners with strong reviews who already invest in marketing and want their ads to reflect why their best-fit couples chose them.",
    "reason_to_care": "Their reviews already describe a valuable client experience. Understanding what couples were weighing before booking can give WIA different information to consider in ad messaging."
  },
  "intended_shift": {
    "understand": "A review of the experience and an account of the booking decision answer different questions.",
    "believe": "The reasons couples chose their business are worth understanding alongside what those couples loved afterward.",
    "do": null
  },
  "point_of_view": "I",
  "wia_perspective": "In a team conversation about our own marketing, I brought up the research we ask new ELEVATE clients to share. Reviews give us useful insight into the experience a business delivers. I also want to understand what a couple was weighing when they decided to book. What mattered to them at that point, and what helped them feel ready to choose you? I recommend asking newly booked couples about that decision. Their answers can give us different information to consider when we write your ad copy and landing pages, alongside what we already know from your reviews.",
  "supporting_example": {
    "scene": "We were talking as a team about improving our own marketing when I brought up a question from ELEVATE onboarding: what research does a new client have to share with us?",
    "wia_move": "I recommended asking newly booked couples what influenced their decision and considering what we learn when we write ad copy and landing pages. I wanted to hear about the moment they chose the business, as well as the experience they reviewed afterward.",
    "client_contribution": "The information I want from couples is what they were considering before booking and what helped them make their choice."
  },
  "narrative_outline": {
    "hooks": [
      {
        "style": "observation",
        "text": "A glowing review can leave an important part of the couple's story untold."
      },
      {
        "style": "question",
        "text": "What would you want to ask a couple right after they chose your business?"
      },
      {
        "style": "story",
        "text": "A conversation about our own marketing brought me back to a question we ask new clients."
      }
    ],
    "problems_desires_symptoms": [
      "Strong reviews already give the owner useful evidence of the experience they deliver.",
      "The owner wants advertising that reflects what matters to best-fit couples.",
      "The booking decision offers another moment to learn from."
    ],
    "main_point": "Show why Whitney recommends asking newly booked couples about their choice and how those answers could inform ad copy and landing pages.",
    "transformation": "Move from treating reviews as the whole story to valuing both the decision to book and the experience afterward.",
    "cta": "No explicit ask. Close on the value of understanding the couple's choice."
  },
  "format_plan": {
    "reels": [
      {
        "job": "Introduce the distinction through Whitney's internal team conversation. One new talking-head Reel is enough for this evidence.",
        "cover_text": "What happened before the review?",
        "spoken_opening": "When a couple books you, what do you want to know about that decision?",
        "script": "When a couple books you, what do you want to know about that decision?\n\nI was talking with our team about our own marketing, and I brought up something we ask new ELEVATE clients: do they have any market research to share with us?\n\nReviews are useful. They help us understand what couples loved about working with you and the experience you delivered.\n\nI'm also interested in what was happening before that experience, when the couple was still deciding who to book.\n\nWhat were they weighing? What helped them feel ready to choose your business for their wedding?\n\nThose are different questions from asking what they loved afterward.\n\nThat's why I recommend asking newly booked couples about their decision, while it's still fresh. Their answers can give us something else to consider when we write your ad copy and landing pages.\n\nWe can look at what mattered to them when they chose you, alongside what your reviews tell us about working with you.\n\nBoth perspectives are valuable. I want to understand the choice as clearly as I understand the experience.",
        "beats": [
          "Open with the question about the booking decision.",
          "Establish the real internal conversation and WIA's onboarding research question.",
          "Explain what reviews reveal, then introduce the earlier decision.",
          "Give Whitney's recommendation without describing a survey system.",
          "Connect the potential information to ad copy and landing pages; close without an ask."
        ],
        "read_time_seconds": 82,
        "source": "new_script"
      }
    ],
    "carousel": {
      "job": "Give the reader a fuller explanation of the two moments and show where the booking-decision question fits into WIA's advertising thinking.",
      "slides": [
        {
          "number": 1,
          "job": "Introduce the two moments without diminishing reviews.",
          "text": "Your couples can tell you about two different moments: choosing you and working with you.\n\nBoth are worth understanding. When I'm thinking about your advertising, I'm interested in what mattered at each point."
        },
        {
          "number": 2,
          "job": "Establish the value of existing reviews.",
          "text": "A thoughtful review gives us insight into the experience you delivered.\n\nIt can help us understand what couples appreciated and what stayed with them after the wedding. That's useful information when we're getting to know your business and thinking about how to communicate its value."
        },
        {
          "number": 3,
          "job": "Locate the additional question before the experience.",
          "text": "The booking decision happened earlier, while the couple was still considering their options.\n\nAt that point, they hadn't experienced everything their review would eventually describe. I want to understand what they were weighing and what helped them feel ready to choose you."
        },
        {
          "number": 4,
          "job": "Show the real WIA contribution.",
          "text": "In a conversation with our team about our own marketing, I brought up the research we ask new ELEVATE clients to share.\n\nThat led me to recommend asking newly booked couples about their decision. It's a moment that can give us different information from a review written after working together."
        },
        {
          "number": 5,
          "job": "Make the recommended inquiry concrete.",
          "text": "I'd want to ask while the choice is still fresh:\n\nWhat were you considering when you decided to book us? What helped you feel this was the right fit for your wedding?\n\nThose questions invite the couple to describe the decision in their own words."
        },
        {
          "number": 6,
          "job": "Connect possible answers to WIA's advertising work.",
          "text": "When we're writing ad copy and landing pages, information about that choice gives us something specific to consider.\n\nWhich parts of the business mattered to the couple before booking? What did they need to understand? Their answers can help us think about what to communicate to couples who are still deciding."
        },
        {
          "number": 7,
          "job": "Explain how the two perspectives complement each other.",
          "text": "I'd consider those answers alongside the reviews.\n\nWhat helped a couple choose you and what they loved afterward may overlap. They may also highlight different parts of your business. Hearing about both moments gives us a fuller picture to work with when we think about your messaging."
        },
        {
          "number": 8,
          "job": "Close on the principle without an ask.",
          "text": "You've earned reviews that speak to the experience you deliver.\n\nThere's also value in hearing how couples arrived at the decision to trust you with their wedding. Understanding that moment gives us another perspective on what your advertising should communicate."
        }
      ]
    },
    "email": {
      "subject_options": [
        "What made them choose you?",
        "The question I'd ask right after a couple books",
        "What a review tells us about your business"
      ],
      "preview": "I'm interested in the booking decision as well as the experience afterward.",
      "opening": "Hi {{contact.first_name}},\n\nWhen I'm thinking about ad messaging, I'm interested in what a couple was considering just before they booked. By the time they write a review, they've experienced your work. At the moment they chose you, they were making a decision about what they hoped that experience would be.",
      "body": "Both moments can tell us something useful.\n\nYour reviews help us understand what couples appreciated about working with you. They give us insight into the experience you deliver and what people remember afterward.\n\nIn a team conversation about improving our own marketing, I brought up the research we ask new ELEVATE clients to share. I recommended asking newly booked couples about what influenced their decision and considering what we learn when we write ad copy and landing pages.\n\nI'd want to understand what they were weighing. What helped them feel ready to choose this business for their wedding? Which parts of what they learned mattered to that choice?\n\nAsking soon after booking gives the couple a chance to talk about the decision while it's still fresh, before the experience itself becomes the focus.\n\nThat's a useful perspective for advertising. We're introducing your business to couples who are still considering their options. Understanding what mattered to people at that same point can give us different information to consider in the copy.\n\nI want to hear those answers alongside what your reviews tell us. The reasons someone chose you and the reasons they loved working with you might reinforce each other, or they might reveal different details worth understanding.\n\nFor me, that's part of being thoughtful about messaging: listening to couples at more than one point in their experience with your business.",
      "ending": "There's value in hearing the story of that choice from the people who made it.\n\nTalk soon,\nWhitney"
    },
    "caption": {
      "hook": "The moment a couple says yes is worth learning from.",
      "body": "When you've delivered a great wedding experience, your reviews give you something valuable to share. They also help us understand what couples appreciated about working with you.\n\nAs we think about your advertising, I want to hear about an earlier moment, too: when those couples were still considering their options.\n\nIn a team conversation about our own marketing, I brought up the research we ask new ELEVATE clients to share. I recommended asking newly booked couples about what influenced their choice and considering what we learn in ad copy and landing pages.\n\nI'd want to know what they were weighing and what helped them feel ready to book. Those questions focus on the decision itself, while it's still fresh.\n\nTheir answers could give us specific priorities to consider when we're introducing your business to other couples. We can think about what mattered before booking alongside what your reviews tell us about the experience afterward.\n\nThat gives us two perspectives on the same business, from people who chose to trust it with their wedding. Both can help us understand what deserves attention in your messaging.",
      "ending": "Choosing you is part of the couple's story, too.\n\n-Whitney"
    },
    "stories": null
  },
